<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ventspils</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Windau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ventspils, German Windau, is a port city at the mouth of the Venta river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>. A Hanseatic town and later the main shipyard of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a>, it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1795 and grew after a railway linked it to the Russian interior in 1901. It remains one of the largest ice-free ports on the Baltic, especially for oil.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ventspils.jpg" alt="Ventspils" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edgars Šulcs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ventspils_viduslaiku_pils_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Ventspils lies in the historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, where the Venta river reaches the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. Its name means castle on the Venta, after the castle that the Livonian Order built beside the river. Historically it was known as Windau in German. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> it was called Vindava or Vindau, and the form Ventspils has been used since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Its harbour does not freeze, which has made it an important port.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town grew up around the Livonian Order's castle and was chartered in 1314. It became an important merchant city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>. Under the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/courland">Courland</a> it flourished as a shipbuilding centre, where 44 warships and 79 trading ships were built, and the duke's fleet that set out to colonise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> sailed from Ventspils. Metal, amber and wood-working shops also contributed to its growth. The Polish-Swedish War and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> destroyed the town, and a plague in 1711 killed most of the remaining inhabitants. After the Third Partition of Poland-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1795 it came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p><p>Shipbuilding and trade did not matter again until about 1850. The port was modernised in the 1890s and linked to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> by rail, and it became one of the most profitable ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Imperial Russia</a>, with a yearly profit of 130 million rubles by 1913. The population grew from 7,000 in 1897 to 29,000 in 1913, then fell by almost half during the German occupation of 1915 to 1919.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> set up a base in 1939, and the city was under Soviet occupation from 1940 and German occupation from 1941 to 1944. In Soviet times an oil pipeline was built to the port, which became the USSR's leading port for crude oil export.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Ventspils is the sixth-largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, and it remains an ice-free port from which oil and other minerals from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> are loaded. It has an international airport and a high technology park. The Old Town keeps 17th-century storehouses near the Market Square, and a house built in 1646 at the crossing of Tirgus and Skolas Streets is among the oldest of its type in Latvia. The city hosts the Sea Festival every July, and since 2006 an International Writers' and Translators' House has stood in the former City Hall of 1850. Ventspils University College is among its institutions of higher education.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ventspils” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Latvian peasants increasingly bought farms, and Libau (Liepāja) and <b>Windau</b> grew as ports.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-courland">Governorate of Courland</a></li></ul></section>
